1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,087
The fear of losing your job can paralyze your best work.

2
00:00:04,037 --> 00:00:09,212
Stop trying to control the outcome of layoffs and start controlling your response.

3
00:00:09,212 --> 00:00:14,887
Uncertainty breeds anxiety, but you can choose to meet it with clarity instead of panic.

4
00:00:14,887 --> 00:00:21,787
Marcus Aurelius taught that external events are beyond our command, but our judgments and actions remain ours.

5
00:00:21,787 --> 00:00:24,537
This is the dichotomy of control.

6
00:00:24,537 --> 00:00:28,987
When you worry about a layoff, you are fighting a battle you cannot win.

7
00:00:28,987 --> 00:00:34,925
Redirect that energy toward what you can actually influence: your preparation and your composure.

8
00:00:34,925 --> 00:00:38,237
Turn this philosophy into a daily routine.

9
00:00:38,237 --> 00:00:43,225
Update your resume and reach out to your network as concrete acts of agency.

10
00:00:43,225 --> 00:00:49,412
In stressful meetings, practice the pause-before-response technique to maintain professional judgment.

11
00:00:49,412 --> 00:00:56,337
Treat every task as an opportunity to demonstrate resilience and keep your mind sharp regardless of external noise.

12
00:00:56,337 --> 00:01:00,737
Subscribe for more practical Stoic tools for modern stress.

